rict (PVI R+1) keeps the race competitive", "Single August poll showed only a narrow 4-point Landsman lead"], "rationale": "Landsman holds a decisive fundraising edge and most raters and prediction markets favor him, but the district's slight Republican lean and the lone poll's narrow margin argue for a competitive rather than comfortable race. A Likely D rating with a mid-single-digit expected margin fits the balance of evidence.", "watch": "Any additional polling showing Conroy closing within a few points, or significant outside spending on his behalf, would move this toward a Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Greg Landsman (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Greg Landsman defends a Cincinnati-area district that leans slightly Republican, against Republican Eric Conroy, a well-funded challenger by local standards who has raised about $1.5 million. Landsman has far out-raised him, holding $3.6 million on hand, and no outside groups have yet engaged heavily on either side.\n\nRaters broadly place the race between Tilt D and Likely D, and prediction markets give Landsman roughly an 87 percent chance. The only public poll, from late August, showed Landsman ahead by four points, suggesting a genuine contest. Data centers and redistricting have featured as campaign issues. The forecast expects Landsman to win by a moderate margin, with meaningful uncertainty given thin polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8833, "margin": 7.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8451, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8473, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8639, "margin": 6.8}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8273, "margin": 6.5}]}